		id: '06-server/08-stack-choice',
		part: 6,
		order: 8,
		title: 'Choosing a stack for a project',
		definition: 'A stack is the set of technologies a service is made of: pages, language, API, sign-in, alerts, data and hosting, chosen by the main action.',
		summary:
			'Everything in this part now comes together in one choice: server, language, API, sign-in and webhooks for a particular project, with the law on personal data and the risks of depending on one provider in mind. Afterwards you’ll have a diagram of the server side and a list of risks.',
		minutes: 20,
		teaches: ['server-stack-choice'],
		requires: ['server-language-niches', 'server-rest', 'server-authentication', 'server-webhooks'],
		comic: {
			panels: [
				{ mood: 'explain', prop: 'flowchart', label: 'form|intake|email|owner', caption: 'An inquiry’s path', say: 'Start with the main action and the path of the data. Each step of the path is a block that needs a tool.' },
				{ mood: 'hint', side: 'right', prop: 'sheet', label: 'no database, no app', say: 'At every step take the simplest thing that fits. A file is enough: no database. A form service is enough: no app.' },
				{ mood: 'hmm', prop: 'network', label: 'a plan for failure', say: 'Every block fails one day. The form is down: the page shows a phone and an email. Alerts down: email still works.' },
				{ mood: 'cheer', side: 'right', prop: 'server', label: '10 components', say: 'Ten components for a small business site, and all ten can break. Complexity has to be earned.' }
			]
		},
		practice: {
			id: 'server-stack',
			title: 'A one-page diagram of your project’s server side',
			task: 'Put the diagram together. 1) In the “Main action” paragraph, name it in the words of your brief and say how it ends. 2) In the list #flow, four steps (the class hop) of the data’s path from the visitor to the owner: name the visitor and the owner. 3) In the table, seven blocks (the class block): pages, language and application, API, sign-in, inquiries, data, hosting. In the “Choice” column, a specific tool or “not needed”; in “Why”, the reason; in one of the reasons, name an item from your list. 4) In the paragraph about personal data, say where it is physically kept and why that matters (the GDPR and UK GDPR, and what they say about transfers abroad). 5) In the list #risks, three risks (the class risk), each with a plan for when the block fails: a backup, a fallback channel, a manual workaround. Every “…” must be replaced.',
			era: 6,
			files: {
				'index.html': `<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    <title>{{title}}</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
  </head>
