			},
			solution: {
				'index.html': `<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    <title>{{title}}</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
  </head>
  <body>
    <header class="site-header" id="top">
      <p class="brand">{{name}}</p>
      <nav aria-label="Site sections">
        <ul>
          <li><a href="#sec-1">{{sections.0}}</a></li>
          <li><a href="#sec-2">{{sections.1}}</a></li>
          <li><a href="#sec-3">{{sections.2}}</a></li>
        </ul>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <main id="main">
      <h1>{{name}}</h1>
      <p>{{who}}</p>
      <p><a class="button" href="#inquiry">{{mainAction.label}}</a></p>

      <section id="sec-1">
        <h2>{{sections.0}}</h2>
        <p>A couple of sentences about this section.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="sec-2">
        <h2>{{sections.1}}</h2>
        <p>A couple of sentences about this section.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="sec-3">
        <h2>{{sections.2}}</h2>
        <p>A couple of sentences about this section.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="offer">
        <h2>What we offer</h2>
        <div class="cards">
          {{#each items}}<article class="card">
            <h3>{{title}}</h3>
            <p>{{text}}</p>
            <p class="price">{{price}}</p>
          </article>
          {{/each}}
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="inquiry">
        <h2>{{mainAction.label}}</h2>
        <form action="/inquiry" method="post">
          <p>
            <label for="f-name">What should we call you?</label><br>
            <input id="f-name" name="name" type="text" autocomplete="name" required>
          </p>
          <p>
            <label for="f-phone">Phone</label><br>
            <input id="f-phone" name="phone" type="tel" autocomplete="tel" required>
          </p>
          <p><button type="submit">{{mainAction.label}}</button></p>
        </form>
      </section>
    </main>


    <section data-scaffold id="stack">
      <h2>My project’s server side</h2>
      <p id="goal">Main action: “{{mainAction.label}}”. The visitor leaves an inquiry, and the owner receives it in Slack and by email.</p>
      <ol id="flow">
        <li class="hop">The visitor opens a page of the site, and nginx serves it as a ready-made file.</li>
        <li class="hop">The visitor fills in the form and sends the inquiry over HTTPS to the API address /api/v1/requests.</li>
        <li class="hop">The application validates the data, saves the inquiry in the database and answers with the code 201.</li>
        <li class="hop">A webhook sends the inquiry to the owner in Slack and by email, and the owner sees it.</li>
      </ol>
      <table>
        <tr><th>Block</th><th>Choice</th><th>Why</th></tr>
        <tr class="block"><td>Pages</td><td class="pick">static files built by a generator, served by nginx</td><td class="why">The sections and the list with “{{items.0.title}}” rarely change: ready-made files are faster and safer.</td></tr>
        <tr class="block"><td>Language and application</td><td class="pick">a small Node.js or PHP application only for receiving inquiries</td><td class="why">Only one place needs to be dynamic, the rest is static.</td></tr>
        <tr class="block"><td>API</td><td class="pick">REST described in OpenAPI</td><td class="why">One list and one inquiry: GraphQL would be overkill.</td></tr>
        <tr class="block"><td>Sign-in</td><td class="pick">not needed for visitors; for the owner a passkey or a link by email</td><td class="why">Visitors need no account, and one person edits the site.</td></tr>
        <tr class="block"><td>Inquiries</td><td class="pick">a webhook into Slack and an email</td><td class="why">The owner learns of an inquiry at once, and the failure of one channel does not lose it.</td></tr>
        <tr class="block"><td>Data</td><td class="pick">a small PostgreSQL database</td><td class="why">Inquiries must be kept somewhere, and a database gives both order and backups.</td></tr>
        <tr class="block"><td>Hosting and server</td><td class="pick">a VPS from a UK or EU provider (for example Hetzner or DigitalOcean)</td><td class="why">One server with nginx is enough, and the data stays in the UK or EEA.</td></tr>
      </table>
      <p id="pd-location">Where personal data is kept: inquiries with names and phone numbers are written to a database on a server in the UK (a UK provider), so the UK GDPR applies and no transfer abroad is needed. A copy in Slack and by email does not replace the main storage, and any processor outside the UK or EEA is covered by a transfer mechanism and named in the privacy notice.</p>
      <ol id="risks">
        <li class="risk">The application with the form is down: the pages stay available, the page shows a phone number and an email for inquiries, and monitoring reports the failure.</li>
        <li class="risk">Slack is unavailable: the inquiry is duplicated by email, so the owner will not lose it.</li>
        <li class="risk">The server is lost: a backup of the database goes elsewhere every day, and the site is redeployed from the repository within an hour.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <footer class="site-footer">
      <p>{{name}}, {{city}}</p>
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>
`,
				'styles.css': `/* styles.css: the page after the CSS part, in short */

*,
*::before,
*::after {
  box-sizing: border-box;
}

body {
  margin: 0;
  font: 1.0625rem/1.6 system-ui, -apple-system, "Segoe UI", Roboto, sans-serif;
  color: #1d2430;
  background-color: #ffffff;
}

main {
  max-width: 50rem;
  margin: 0 auto;
  padding: 1.5rem 1rem;
}

h1,
h2,
h3 {
  line-height: 1.2;
}

a {
  color: #1a4d8f;
}

nav ul {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 0.5rem 1rem;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.site-header {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: space-between;
  gap: 0.75rem 1.5rem;
  padding: 1rem;
  color: #ffffff;
  background-color: #1a4d8f;
}

.site-header a {
  color: #ffffff;
}

.brand {
  margin: 0;
  font-size: 1.5rem;
}

.button {
  display: inline-block;
  min-height: 2.75rem;
  padding: 0.625rem 1.25rem;
  color: #ffffff;
  background-color: #1a4d8f;
  border-radius: 0.25rem;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.cards {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(14rem, 1fr));
  gap: 1rem;
}

.card {
  padding: 1rem;
  background-color: #f3f5f8;
  border-radius: 0.5rem;
}

.price {
  font-weight: 600;
}

input,
button {
  font: inherit;
}

input {
  min-height: 2.75rem;
  padding: 0 0.75rem;
}

.site-footer {
  padding: 1rem;
  background-color: #f3f5f8;
}
`
			},
			checks: [
				{
					id: 'stack-section',
					kind: 'exists',
					selector: '#stack'
				},
				{
					id: 'no-blanks',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#stack',
					matches: '^[^…]*$'
				},
				{
					id: 'goal-label',
					kind: 'field',
					selector: '#goal',
					field: 'mainAction.label'
				},
				{
					id: 'four-hops',
					kind: 'count',
					selector: '#flow li.hop',
					min: 4
				},
				{
					id: 'flow-ends',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#flow',
					matches: '^(?=[\\s\\S]*[Vv]isitor)(?=[\\s\\S]*(?:[Oo]wner|[Aa]dmin|[Mm]anager))'
				},
				{
					id: 'seven-blocks',
					kind: 'count',
					selector: '#stack tr.block',
					min: 7
				},
				{
					id: 'concrete-picks',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#stack td.pick',
					matches: 'nginx|Caddy|Apache|PHP|Python|Node|Go\\b|Elixir|Ruby|Java|WordPress|Drupal|Wix|Squarespace|Shopify|REST|GraphQL|OpenAPI|PostgreSQL|SQLite|Slack|Telegram|[Ee]mail|not needed|passkey|Google|Hetzner|DigitalOcean|AWS|Netlify|Vercel|Cloudflare|static'
				},
				{
					id: 'mentions-item',
					kind: 'field',
					selector: '#stack td.why',
					field: 'items[].title',
					min: 1,
					when: {
						brief: 'items[].title'
					}
				},
				{
					id: 'pd-location',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#pd-location',
					matches: '^(?=[\\s\\S]*(?:\\b(?:UK|EU|EEA)\\b|Europe|United Kingdom|Britain))(?=[\\s\\S]*(?:GDPR|law|transfer))'
				},
				{
					id: 'three-risks',
					kind: 'count',
					selector: '#risks li.risk',
					min: 3
				},
				{
					id: 'risk-plans',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#risks',
					matches: 'backup|copy|fallback|spare|plan|switch|manual|duplicat|[Ee]mail'
				}
			]
		},
		quiz: [
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'Where should the choice of a stack for a project begin?',
				options: [
					'With the visitor’s main action and the path of the data from them to the owner: then find tools for that',
					'With the trendiest language',
					'With the most expensive hosting'
				],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. A stack serves a task: once you know an inquiry must reach the owner, you can see which blocks are needed and which are surplus.',
					'No: fashion passes, and the project is left with the wrong tool.',
					'No: the price of hosting has nothing to do with what the project needs.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'multiple',
				prompt: 'Which of these does a small business site with an inquiry form need?',
				options: [
					'Hosting or a server for the static pages',
					'A way to receive an inquiry: an application or a form service',
					'A notification to the owner about an inquiry',
					'A Kubernetes cluster'
				],
				answer: [0, 1, 2],
				why: [
					'Yes: pages need an address from which they are served.',
					'Yes: a form needs a receiver, however small.',
					'Yes: an inquiry nobody hears about is lost.',
					'No: Kubernetes for a small business site is complexity that will not pay for itself. It comes up in the part on infrastructure.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'Inquiries with the name and phone number of people in the UK or the EU are best stored...',
				options: [
					'In a database with a provider you have a contract with, in the UK or EEA or under a lawful transfer mechanism, and described in the privacy notice',
					'Only in a chat app: it is cheaper',
					'Anywhere: the law says nothing about it'
				],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. The GDPR and UK GDPR require a contract with each processor, and a lawful basis for sending data outside the UK or EEA (an adequacy decision, standard contractual clauses and so on). Say where the data goes in the privacy notice. Details are worth discussing with a lawyer, or the regulator.',
					'No: a chat app is not a store, and its servers may be outside your country.',
					'No: the law speaks about it directly, and fines for breaking it exist.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'What does “a plan for when a block fails” mean?',
				options: [
					'It is known in advance what to do if a block breaks: a fallback channel, a backup, a manual workaround',
					'A plan to replace all the blocks once a year',
					'A hope that the block will not break'
				],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. Everything fails, and the question is not “if” but “when”. A good plan is short: “we duplicate the inquiry by email”, “the copy sits somewhere else”.',
					'No: that is an upgrade plan, not a response plan.',
					'No: hope is not a plan.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'What is risky about relying on a single outside service for an important block (taking payments, say, or forms)?',
				options: [
					'It can become unavailable, change its terms or its prices, or stop working in your country: a fallback is needed',
					'Outside services are always unreliable',
					'Nothing: they are better than anything you could build'
				],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. Terms, prices and availability of a service change quickly, and not at your wish. For every important block think of a replacement, so that moving takes days, not months.',
					'No: the point is not quality but dependence on terms you do not control.',
					'No: “better” does not answer the question about risk.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'order',
				prompt: 'Put the steps of choosing a stack in order',
				items: [
					'Write down the visitor’s main action',
					'Draw the data’s path from the visitor to the owner',
					'Pick the simplest suitable tool for every block of the path',
					'Check where the personal data is physically kept',
					'Name the risks and a plan for the failure of each block'
				]
			}
		],
		cards: [
			{
				id: 'server-stack-start',
				front: 'Where do you start when choosing a stack?',
				back: 'With the visitor’s main action and the path of the data to the owner. For each step of the path pick the simplest suitable tool, and cross out whatever isn’t needed.'
			},
			{
				id: 'server-stack-blocks',
				front: 'What blocks does the server side consist of?',
				back: 'Pages (static or dynamic), language and application, API, sign-in, receiving inquiries and notifications, data storage, hosting and server. Some blocks may be “not needed”, and that is fine.'
			},
			{
				id: 'server-stack-pd',
				front: 'Where do you keep visitors’ personal data?',
				back: 'With a processor you have a contract with, and where the law allows. Under the GDPR and UK GDPR, sending data outside the UK or EEA needs a transfer mechanism, and the privacy notice says where it goes. Russia is stricter: the first recording of Russian citizens’ data must be made in Russia (152-FZ).'
			},
			{
				id: 'server-stack-risks',
				front: 'How should you treat the risks of a stack?',
				back: 'For each important block, name in advance what happens when it fails, and a plan: a backup somewhere else, a fallback notification channel, a manual workaround, a spare provider.'
			},
			{
				id: 'server-stack-boring',
				front: 'Why is a “boring” stack often better?',
				back: 'Proven tools are easier to maintain, and it is easier to find people and answers for them. Take something new when it solves a problem the boring option cannot.'
			}
		],
		sources: [
			{
				title: 'The UK GDPR: Regulation (EU) 2016/679 as it forms part of UK law (legislation.gov.uk)',
				url: 'https://www.legislation.gov.uk/eur/2016/679/contents',
				licence: 'link-only',
				kind: 'spec',
				lang: 'en'
			},
			{
				title: 'ICO: International transfers',
				url: 'https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/international-transfers/',
				licence: 'link-only',
				kind: 'docs',
				lang: 'en'
			},
			{
				title: 'EDPB: International data transfers (guide for small businesses)',
				url: 'https://www.edpb.europa.eu/sme/be-compliant/international-data-transfers_en',
				licence: 'link-only',
				kind: 'docs',
				lang: 'en'
			},
			{
				title: 'Stack Overflow Developer Survey 2025',
				url: 'https://survey.stackoverflow.co/2025/',
				licence: 'link-only',
				kind: 'article',
				lang: 'en'
			},
			{
				title: 'Federal Law of 27.07.2006 No. 152-FZ “On Personal Data” (Russia, for comparison)',
				url: 'https://www.consultant.ru/document/cons_doc_LAW_61801/',
				licence: 'link-only',
				kind: 'spec',
				lang: 'ru'
			}
		],
